        <body>
            <div xml:id="d234">

                <pb facs="0381" n="287" xml:id="pg287"/>

                <head>No. 234.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Bayard to Mr. McLane</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1887-02-01"><hi rendition="#italic">February</hi> 1,
                        1887</date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 194.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Your dispatch. No. 346, of the 13th ultimo,
                    in regard to the application of Mr. Richard King (who has made his declaration
                    of his intention to become an American citizen) for a passport has been received
                    and considered.</p>
                <p>If Mr. King should, on appealing to this Government for protection, show that he
                    was domiciled in this country as well as an inchoate citizen by virtue of having
                    declared his intention, the question of granting protection would be presented
                    for consideration. But this position does not involve the admission of Mr.
                    King’s right to a passport or special protection papers. A passport can only be
                    granted to native or naturalized citizens, and protection papers are no longer
                    issued by the Department.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">T. F. Bayard.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
